pages.content:
The **`==` operator** checks **reference equality** (memory addresses), while **`.equals()`** checks **value equality** (content).

### Reference Equality and `.intern()`

```java
public class ReferenceEqualityDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String literal1 = "Code";
        String literal2 = "Code";
        String heapString = new String("Code");

        // Literals share the same pool address
        System.out.println("literal1 == literal2: " + (literal1 == literal2)); // true

        // Heap object has a separate address
        System.out.println("literal1 == heapString: " + (literal1 == heapString)); // false

        // .intern() retrieves the canonical pool reference
        String interned = heapString.intern();
        System.out.println("literal1 == interned: " + (literal1 == interned)); // true
    }
}
```

### Summary Comparison Table

| Operator / Method | What It Checks | Example `"Hi" == new String("Hi")` | Example `"Hi".equals(new String("Hi"))` |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`==`** | Heap Memory Address | `false` | N/A |
| **`.equals()`** | Character Sequence | N/A | `true` |
| **`.equalsIgnoreCase()`** | Case-Insensitive Characters | N/A | `true` (e.g. `"HI"` vs `"hi"`) |

codeCard.code:
public class StringInterningDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s1 = new String("pool");
        String s2 = s1.intern(); // Fetch canonical reference from String pool
        String s3 = "pool";

        System.out.println("s1 == s2: " + (s1 == s2)); // false
        System.out.println("s2 == s3: " + (s2 == s3)); // true (both point to pooled object)
    }
}

