pages.content:
The **`indexOf(String substr)`** method searches for the first occurrence of a substring within the calling string, returning the **0-based index** of its first character.

### Method Overloads

1. **`indexOf(String str)`**: Searches forward from index 0.
2. **`indexOf(String str, int fromIndex)`**: Searches forward starting from the specified `fromIndex`.

```java
public class IndexOfStringDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String sentence = "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog";

        int firstThe = sentence.indexOf("The");
        System.out.println("Index of 'The': " + firstThe); // 0

        int secondThe = sentence.indexOf("the");
        System.out.println("Index of 'the': " + secondThe); // 31

        int fox = sentence.indexOf("fox");
        System.out.println("Index of 'fox': " + fox);       // 16

        int cat = sentence.indexOf("cat");
        System.out.println("Index of 'cat': " + cat);       // -1 (not found)

        // Searching with fromIndex:
        String text = "banana";
        int firstAn = text.indexOf("an");     // 1
        int nextAn = text.indexOf("an", 2);   // 3
        System.out.println("First 'an': " + firstAn + ", Next 'an': " + nextAn);
    }
}
```

> [!NOTE]
> `indexOf()` is case-sensitive. If the substring is not found, it returns **`-1`**.

