pages.content:
The **`contains(CharSequence s)`** method returns a boolean indicating whether the specified sequence of characters exists anywhere within the calling string.

### Syntax and Characteristics

```java
boolean exists = str.contains(CharSequence sequence);
```

- **Return Type:** Primitive `boolean` (`true` or `false`).
- **Internal Mechanism:** In OpenJDK, `str.contains(s)` delegates directly to `str.indexOf(s.toString()) >= 0`.
- **Case Sensitivity:** It is strictly case-sensitive. `"Java".contains("java")` returns `false`.

```java
public class ContainsDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String email = "support@company.com";

        // Validating email domain
        if (email.contains("@") && email.contains(".")) {
            System.out.println("Basic email format valid.");
        }

        // Case sensitivity check
        String title = "Java Programming Masterclass";
        System.out.println("Contains 'Java': " + title.contains("Java")); // true
        System.out.println("Contains 'java': " + title.contains("java")); // false

        // Case-insensitive check technique:
        boolean caseInsensitive = title.toLowerCase().contains("java");
        System.out.println("Case-insensitive contains 'java': " + caseInsensitive); // true
    }
}
```

