pages.content:
The `toLowerCase()` method in Java converts **all characters in a string to lowercase**. This method does not take any input parameters and is useful for formatting text, standardizing input, and ensuring case-insensitive comparisons.

**Syntax:**

```java
String lowerString = originalString.toLowerCase();
```

---

**Example:**

```java
class Seedit {
 public static void main(String[] args) {
 // Declare and initialize a string
 String message = "HELLO WORLD";

 // Convert the string to lowercase
 String lowerMessage = message.toLowerCase();

 // Print the result
 System.out.println("Lowercase Message: " + lowerMessage);
 }
}
```


**Expected Output:**

```default
Lowercase Message: hello world
```

codeCard.code:
public class LowerCaseDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String rawEmail = "Alice.WONDERLAND_99@Example.COM";

        // Normalize email to lowercase for case-insensitive comparison
        String normalizedEmail = rawEmail.toLowerCase();

        System.out.println("Raw Input: " + rawEmail);
        System.out.println("Normalized: " + normalizedEmail);

        // Case-insensitive verification
        boolean matches = normalizedEmail.equals("alice.wonderland_99@example.com");
        System.out.println("Matches system record: " + matches);
    }
}

pages.content:
The `trim()` method in Java removes **leading and trailing spaces** from a string. It is useful when processing user input, formatting data, or comparing strings without unwanted whitespace.

**Syntax:**
```java
String trimmedString = originalString.trim();
```

---

**Example:**

```java
class Seedit {
 public static void main(String[] args) {
 // Declare and initialize a string with leading and trailing spaces
 String message = " Hello, World! ";

 // Remove leading and trailing spaces
 String trimmedMessage = message.trim();

 // Print the result
 System.out.println("Trimmed Message: [" + trimmedMessage + "]");
 }
}
```

**Expected Output:**
```default
Trimmed Message: [Hello, World!]
```

---

The trim() method ensures that unnecessary spaces do not interfere with string operations such as comparison and storage.

codeCard.code:
public class TrimDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String input = "   Java Programming Language   ";

        String clean = input.trim();

        System.out.println("Before: [" + input + "]");
        System.out.println("After:  [" + clean + "]");
        System.out.println("Original length: " + input.length());
        System.out.println("Cleaned length:  " + clean.length());
    }
}

