The static `String.join()` method (introduced in Java 8) provides an elegant, clean way to join elements without manual loop counters or trailing delimiter trimming.

### Method Signatures

Java provides two overloaded static `join` methods:
1. **Varargs:**
   ```java
   public static String join(CharSequence delimiter, CharSequence... elements)
   ```
2. **Iterable Collection:**
   ```java
   public static String join(CharSequence delimiter, Iterable<? extends CharSequence> elements)
   ```

### Handling Null Elements
If an element in the elements sequence is `null`, `String.join()` appends the literal string `"null"`. However, if the `delimiter` itself is `null`, a `NullPointerException` is thrown.

### Example: Building SQL Clauses and CSV Lines
```java
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class JoinAdvancedDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> columns = Arrays.asList("id", "first_name", "last_name", "email");
        String sqlSelect = "SELECT " + String.join(", ", columns) + " FROM users;";
        System.out.println(sqlSelect);
        // SELECT id, first_name, last_name, email FROM users;

        // Joining array elements
        String[] tags = {"java", "springboot", "backend"};
        String tagString = String.join("#", "#" + tags[0], tags[1], tags[2]);
        System.out.println(String.join(" #", tags));
    }
}
```

