### Common String Mistakes and How to Prevent Them

Beginner errors in Java String manipulation typically stem from misconceptions about memory, indexing, or immutability.

#### Mistake 1: Off-By-One Loop Condition
```java
// BUG: Using <= s.length()
for (int i = 0; i <= s.length(); i++) {
    System.out.println(s.charAt(i)); // Throws StringIndexOutOfBoundsException at i == s.length()
}

// FIX: Always use < s.length()
for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
    System.out.println(s.charAt(i));
}
```

#### Mistake 2: Ignoring String Immutability
```java
// BUG: Believing toLowerCase() mutates s
String s = "HELLO";
s.toLowerCase();
// s is STILL "HELLO"!

// FIX: Always capture the returned reference
s = s.toLowerCase();
```

#### Mistake 3: Comparing Strings with `==`
```java
// BUG: == compares memory references, not text content!
String userEntered = scanner.next();
if (userEntered == "admin") { ... } // Almost always evaluates to false!

// FIX: Always use .equals() or .equalsIgnoreCase()
if ("admin".equalsIgnoreCase(userEntered)) { ... } // Also prevents NPE if userEntered is null!
```

codeCard.code:
public class StringPitfallsFixes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Pitfall 1: Immutability reassignment
        String message = "hello world";
        message.toUpperCase(); // Lost return value!
        System.out.println("Without reassignment: " + message); // hello world
        message = message.toUpperCase(); // Correct
        System.out.println("With reassignment:    " + message); // HELLO WORLD

        // Pitfall 2: Safe string comparison
        String input = null;
        // Bad: input.equals("exit") -> NullPointerException!
        // Good: "exit".equalsIgnoreCase(input) -> false (safe!)
        boolean isExit = "exit".equalsIgnoreCase(input);
        System.out.println("Safe comparison: " + isExit);
    }
}

