### Common Mistakes in Substring & Builder Operations

#### Mistake 1: Comparing StringBuilder Objects with .equals()
```java
StringBuilder sb1 = new StringBuilder("java");
StringBuilder sb2 = new StringBuilder("java");

// BUG: StringBuilder does NOT override Object.equals()!
System.out.println(sb1.equals(sb2)); // PRINTS FALSE!

// FIX: Convert to String first, or compare contents
System.out.println(sb1.toString().equals(sb2.toString())); // PRINTS TRUE!
// Or in Java 11+: System.out.println(sb1.compareTo(sb2) == 0);
```

#### Mistake 2: Forgetting to Check if indexOf() Returned -1
```java
String filename = "document";
int dotIndex = filename.indexOf('.'); // Returns -1!

// BUG: Passing -1 into substring
String ext = filename.substring(dotIndex + 1); // substring(0) -> returns "document"!

// FIX: Check for -1 explicitly
String ext = (dotIndex != -1) ? filename.substring(dotIndex + 1) : "";
```

#### Mistake 3: Inverted Substring Indices
```java
// BUG: beginIndex > endIndex
String s = "hello";
s.substring(3, 1); // Throws StringIndexOutOfBoundsException!
```

codeCard.code:
public class StringBuilderEqualsPitfall {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        StringBuilder sb1 = new StringBuilder("seed");
        StringBuilder sb2 = new StringBuilder("seed");

        // Pitfall: StringBuilder does NOT override equals()
        System.out.println("sb1.equals(sb2): " + sb1.equals(sb2)); // false!

        // Proper fix 1: Convert to String before calling equals()
        System.out.println("toString().equals: " + sb1.toString().equals(sb2.toString())); // true

        // Proper fix 2: Use compareTo() (implements Comparable since Java 11)
        System.out.println("compareTo == 0:    " + (sb1.compareTo(sb2) == 0)); // true
    }
}

